-## 4. Deploying on Render — flat, plain Python, already has your API key
+## 4. Slack alerts for flagged transactions
+
+Every open transaction the model scores at or above `SLACK_ALERT_THRESHOLD`
+(default `0.7`, i.e. 70% predicted bank-RFI risk) gets posted to Slack —
+transaction ID, amount, counterparty country, risk score, and the top reasons
+the model flagged it. Each transaction is only posted once (tracked via an
+internal `slack_alerted` flag), not again on every subsequent hourly retrain
+while its score stays elevated.
+
+Three delivery methods are supported, tried in this order:
+
+1. **Slack Web API with a rotating (expiring) token pair** — used if
+   `SLACK_CLIENT_ID`, `SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET`, `SLACK_ACCESS_TOKEN`, and
+   `SLACK_REFRESH_TOKEN` are all set. This is what a Slack app with **Token
+   Rotation** enabled issues: an access token (`xoxe.xoxb-...` for a bot, or
+   `xoxe.xoxp-...` for a user) that expires in ~12 hours, paired with a
+   refresh token (`xoxe-1-...`). The bot automatically exchanges the
+   refresh token for a new pair before the access token expires — no manual
+   re-pasting of tokens needed after the initial setup.
+2. **Slack Web API with a static (non-expiring) bot token** — used if
+   `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` and `SLACK_CHANNEL` are set (and method 1 isn't
+   configured). The classic Bot User OAuth Token (`xoxb-...`) — simpler if
+   your Slack app doesn't have Token Rotation turned on.
+3. **Incoming Webhook** — used if `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` is set (and neither
+   API method above is configured). Simplest: one URL tied to one
+   pre-chosen channel, no app/bot/scopes/tokens to manage at all.
+
+If none of the above is configured, alerting is a silent no-op — nothing
+breaks, it just doesn't post.
+
+**Setup — rotating token method (use this if your Slack app has Token
+Rotation enabled, i.e. you were issued an access token *and* a refresh
+token):**
+
+1. In your Slack app's **Basic Information** page, under **App
+   Credentials**, copy the **Client ID** and **Client Secret**.
+2. Get an initial access token + refresh token pair for the `chat:write`
+   scope (via your app's OAuth install flow, or however you originally
+   obtained the pair) — the access token starts `xoxe.xoxb-...` or
+   `xoxe.xoxp-...`, the refresh token starts `xoxe-1-...`.
+3. In Slack, go to the target channel (e.g. `#potential-bank-rfi-alerts`)
+   and invite the bot: `/invite @YourBotName` (it can only post where it's
+   been invited).
+4. On Render: **Environment** tab → add all four of `SLACK_CLIENT_ID`,
+   `SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET`, `SLACK_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `SLACK_REFRESH_TOKEN`, plus
+   `SLACK_CHANNEL` (e.g. `#potential-bank-rfi-alerts`).
+5. Redeploy, then verify with `POST /api/slack/test` (see below).
+
+**Important:** `SLACK_ACCESS_TOKEN`/`SLACK_REFRESH_TOKEN` are only the
+*starting* pair. The very first time the bot sends a Slack message, it
+immediately refreshes them (to establish a known expiry) and stores the
+live pair in its own database — Slack invalidates each refresh token the
+moment it's used and issues a brand-new one, so the original env var values
+stop working after that first refresh. This is expected; you don't need to
+update the env vars again afterward.
+
+**Setup — static token method (simpler, if Token Rotation isn't on for your
+app):**
+
+1. In Slack: **api.slack.com/apps → Create New App → From scratch** → pick
+   your workspace and give it a name (e.g. "Bank RFI Bot").
+2. **OAuth & Permissions** → under **Scopes → Bot Token Scopes**, add
+   `chat:write` → **Install to Workspace** (or **Install App**) → copy the
+   **Bot User OAuth Token** (starts with `xoxb-`).
+3. In Slack itself, invite the bot into the target channel:
+   `/invite @Bank RFI Bot`.
+4. On Render: add `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` (the `xoxb-...` value) and
+   `SLACK_CHANNEL`.
+5. Redeploy, then verify with `POST /api/slack/test`.
+
+**Setup — Incoming Webhook method (simplest, no app management):**
+
+1. In Slack: **api.slack.com/apps → Create New App → From scratch** → pick
+   your workspace → **Incoming Webhooks** → toggle **Activate Incoming
+   Webhooks** on → **Add New Webhook to Workspace** → choose the channel →
+   copy the generated webhook URL (`https://hooks.slack.com/services/...`).
+2. On Render: **Environment** tab → add `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` with that value.
+3. Redeploy, then verify with `POST /api/slack/test`.
+
+Whichever method you use, optionally add `SLACK_ALERT_THRESHOLD` to change
+the `0.7` default. Verify with `POST /api/slack/test` (e.g. via the
+dashboard's browser console or
+`curl -X POST https://<your-render-url>/api/slack/test`) — it sends one
+harmless confirmation message to the channel. `GET /api/setup` also reports
+`slack_configured` and `slack_mode` (`api_rotating` / `api_static` /
+`webhook` / `none`) so you can confirm which path is active.
+
+Alerts fire automatically after every retrain — both the real-time one
+triggered by a Sumsub webhook event and the hourly safety-net cycle — so no
+separate polling job is needed.
+
+### Slack environment variables
+
+| Variable | Required? | Default | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `SLACK_CLIENT_ID` | No (rotating method) | — | Slack app's Client ID, from Basic Information → App Credentials. Needed to refresh the rotating token pair. |
+| `SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET` | No (rotating method) | — | Slack app's Client Secret, from Basic Information → App Credentials. |
+| `SLACK_ACCESS_TOKEN` | No (rotating method) | — | Starting rotating access token (`xoxe.xoxb-...` / `xoxe.xoxp-...`). Only used once, to bootstrap the first automatic refresh. |
+| `SLACK_REFRESH_TOKEN` | No (rotating method) | — | Starting refresh token (`xoxe-1-...`). Only used once; a new one is issued (and stored internally) on every refresh after that. |
+| `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` | No (static method) | — | Classic, non-expiring bot token (`xoxb-...`) with `chat:write` scope. Alternative to the rotating pair above. |
+| `SLACK_CHANNEL` | No (either API method) | — | Channel name (e.g. `#potential-bank-rfi-alerts`) or ID to post to via the API. |
+| `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` | No (webhook method) | — | Slack Incoming Webhook URL. Used only if neither API method above is configured. |
+| `SLACK_ALERT_THRESHOLD` | No | `0.7` | Minimum predicted risk score (0–1) that triggers a Slack post. |
+
